  "schematicNotes": [
    {
      "id": "n_scope",
      "width": 390,
      "schematicPosition": {
        "x": 1920,
        "y": 0
      },
      "text": "REAR WHEEL SPEED. RL and RR ABS drops run as two separate legs to the rear VRC IN M8 plugs; the VRC sits mid-dash above the ECU beside the front VRC (Route 10 rev 2). One screened 4-core output cable runs to the 6-way inline DT04-6P near the ECU (IX_WS_REAR). The ECU side owns only the short spur and the DT06-6S mating half (ST185-B-cabin). No bulkhead."
    },
    {
      "id": "n_vrc",
      "width": 390,
      "color": "Orange",
      "schematicPosition": {
        "x": 1920,
        "y": 210
      },
      "text": "VRC NOT DRAWN. The VR conditioner, its enclosure and its panel connectors belong to the enclosure build, not this harness. The two IN M8 cable plugs (m8_vrc_r_inl / m8_vrc_r_inr, binder 99 3363 100 04: pin 1 VR+, pin 2 VR-, pin 3 screen to the case, pin 4 spare) ARE drawn on this harness (Daniel 2026-10-12). The OUT side is still the loose device endpoint VRC_REAR_OUT. RL and RR run as separate legs to the box - no shared Y."
    },
    {
      "id": "n_shield",
      "width": 390,
      "color": "Orange",
      "schematicPosition": {
        "x": 1920,
        "y": 420
      },
      "text": "SHIELDS - VRC powered-device exception (docs/SHIELD-RULES.md 6.30). Each sensor screen floats at the sensor and lands on its M8 IN plug pin 3 (to the case inside the box). The output cable screen is bonded to the VRC case and runs to inline pin 5, then on the spur to SHIELD_B (B17). One screen node, grounded once, at the ECU."
    },
    {
      "id": "n_iso",
      "width": 390,
      "color": "Red",
      "schematicPosition": {
        "x": 1920,
        "y": 630
      },
      "text": "TWO ISOLATION RULES. 1. The enclosure must not touch chassis - it is part of the screen, not a ground. Nylon mounting hardware. 2. The PCB must not touch the enclosure - nylon standoffs. The board grounds through the output cable only. If either is bonded, the screen picks up supply-ground noise and it will read as a flaky sensor."
    },
    {
      "id": "n_coat",
      "width": 390,
      "color": "Red",
      "schematicPosition": {
        "x": 1920,
        "y": 810
      },
      "text": "MASK THE THREE CONNECTOR LANDINGS AND THE CASE RING-TERMINAL SPOT BEFORE POWDER COAT. The OUT shielding plate and the input screen ring terminals need bare metal. A coated landing silently breaks the screen."
    }
  ],
